<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Spec 26, Sprint 2.

  The home server is served over plain HTTP on the LAN. Android blocks cleartext
  by default from API 28, so without this the app cannot reach it at all: every
  fetch fails, and Filesystem.downloadFile fails too, since it obeys the same
  policy.

  Why blanket cleartextTrafficPermitted rather than an allow-list: the server
  address is user-configurable and DHCP-assigned, so a domain-config list would
  break the moment the router hands out a new IP. That is exactly the problem the
  in-app settings screen was added to solve. IP ranges cannot be expressed in
  this file either, only hosts.

  This is real security debt, recorded in LIMITATIONS.md. It is acceptable here
  because the only cleartext traffic the app generates is to the server the user
  typed in, over a home LAN, and the alternative (TLS with a self-signed
  certificate) trades a real risk for a support burden on a household tool. If
  the app is ever exposed beyond a trusted LAN, this must become an allow-list
  and the server must serve TLS.

  Note: XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, which is why the em dashes
  in this prose are written as "that is".
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>